WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.200
-My first guest is
a multiple Oscar-winning actor

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.067
and a legendary New Yorker.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.934
He's a co-founder
of the Tribeca Film Festival,

00:00:08.934 --> 00:00:11.133
which celebrates
its 20th anniversary

00:00:11.133 --> 00:00:12.434
starting this Wednesday.

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:14.934
Here is Robert De Niro.

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:17.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:21.367
Hey, buddy. Oh, my God.
I love you, buddy!

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:24.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:26.501
We have a full --

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:31.200
It's our first full audience
in over a year.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.400
-Wow. Oh.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.467
In over a year, this is
the first full audience?

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.868
-Yeah, this is it.
-Great, great.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:38.567
-Yeah.

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.100
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.734
-I'm sorry I can't be there.

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:44.601
-No. Look. We're happy
to see you and talk to you.

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:45.734
Thanks so much for being here.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.968
Every time.
I love seeing you, buddy.

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:50.200
The Tribeca Film Festival
this Wednesday

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.300
kicks off with "In the Heights."

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.601
Which is unbelievable,
by the way. It's fantastic.

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:57.701
It is perfect for
the Tribeca Film Festival.

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.100
It's been 20 years

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.567
since and you Jane Rosenthal
thought of this.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:03.801
Did you ever imagine
it would be this big

00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:06.734
or end up being this big?
-No.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.801
I mean, we were just
thinking of it year by year

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.534
in the beginning,

00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:15.067
and then it -- it took off.

00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:17.801
But who can predict
these types of things?

00:01:17.801 --> 00:01:19.000
-Yeah, we talk about this.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.734
You're nice enough
to come on our show

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:23.400
and talk about it every year.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.601
We go through the good moments,

00:01:24.601 --> 00:01:27.367
and the one you said
that sticks in your mind

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:31.734
is having Nelson Mandela
and him speaking about movies.

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:33.200
And it stuck in your mind,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.167
the healing properties
of movies and how --

00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:37.434
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:37.434 --> 00:01:39.501
I was going to ask you,
is there a film with --

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.234
-No. Just -- Just --
-Is there --

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:44.400
-Go, go, go. Sorry.
I'm telling somebody

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.801
to leave the room
because they're distracting me.

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:47.968
Sorry.

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:50.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:53.100
I'm trying to do...

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.501
but my hands
are going like this.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.267
-Get out of the room!
Get out! We're doing

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.968
"The Tonight Show"
for goodness --

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:01.400
But it's the first time
you've had a full audience

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.133
in your living room.
It's amazing.

00:02:03.133 --> 00:02:05.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:08.968
-Yeah, it's a more
intimate situation, actually,

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:13.000
one-on-one, for me,
where I am in my living room.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.701
So having somebody there,
it just feels better this way.

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:18.234
-I think this is special for me

00:02:18.234 --> 00:02:20.601
because I think this probably
will be the last time

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:22.334
we'll ever FaceTime
with each other

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:24.901
'cause next time
it will be in person.

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:26.567
-Yes, yes.
-Yeah, so this is good.

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:28.400
We're making
a little history tonight.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.834
-Yeah.
-Yeah. Uh...

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:33.267
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:36.033
Yeah. I can tell
you're excited as much as I am.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.434
-[ Laughter ]
-I am.

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:40.167
-No. It's alright. Hold it back.
Hold it back. Alright. Yeah.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:42.334
Let's bring that person
back into the room.

00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:44.267
-No. No. I'm very happy.
Don't worry.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.100
-I know you are. I know, buddy.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.834
Now, you're taking
this Tribeca Film Festival --

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:54.234
You're doing this thing called
"Borough by Borough."

00:02:54.234 --> 00:02:57.133
And you're having
these outdoor screenings.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:59.801
Like, virtual --
Like, these awesome

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:02.901
40-foot L.E.D. drive-ins,
basically.

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:05.167
-Yes.
-I remember -- The first year

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:09.133
you started getting
into virtual-reality stuff.

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.901
Like, not just movies.
Breaking out --

00:03:10.901 --> 00:03:14.334
Also embracing -- This year,
you're embracing video games,

00:03:14.334 --> 00:03:15.701
which I think is very smart.

00:03:15.701 --> 00:03:17.267
-Yes.
-It's brilliant. I love it.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:19.033
-And I played
every one of those games.

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:20.300
-Of course you do.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.334
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.467
Remember that one time you did
a virtual-reality thing?

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:26.834
I think it was your first
virtual-reality thing.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:29.067
And you were in -- Your first
virtual-reality thing,

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:30.767
you were in a prison cell.

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:33.567
Do you remember this?

00:03:33.567 --> 00:03:35.601
-I -- Was it with you
or at the festival?

00:03:35.601 --> 00:03:36.968
-I don't know.

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:38.601
I think we might have been
together or something weird,

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:40.300
but we put on goggles and we
were sitting in a prison cell.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.400
-Oh, okay, yeah.
Right, right, right.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.234
-Out of all the things
we could have done,

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:46.501
it was just very interesting.
Why did we do that?

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:48.934
But it felt like
we went to prison together.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:51.300
Anyway, we served time.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.601
While I have you here, there's
a lot of myths about you.

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:57.334
You have a lot of mystique
around you.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:04:00.133
And I was wondering if I could
ask you a couple stories

00:04:00.133 --> 00:04:03.033
and see if they're true or not,
if you wouldn't mind doing that.

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.667
-Sure.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:06.501
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:07.968
-The first one...

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:09.234
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.234 --> 00:04:10.334
[ Applause ]

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:11.934
-The first one --
-No!

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:14.934
-No.
-You have to say yes!

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:17.267
You have to say yes!
No, you have to!

00:04:17.267 --> 00:04:19.667
It's our first audience.
It's our first audience.

00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:22.601
-Okay, okay.

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:24.968
-You won your first Oscar
for "Godfather II"

00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:27.334
playing a young Vito Corleone.

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:30.033
But that almost never happened
because you actually auditioned

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:33.367
for the role of Sonny Corleone
in the first "Godfather,"

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:35.167
which went to James Caan.

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:36.767
Is that true?
-Yes.

00:04:36.767 --> 00:04:39.234
Yes. I --

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.501
Everybody was up for Michael,

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:45.067
but everybody knew
that Al was gonna do it,

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.767
that Francis wanted him.

00:04:46.767 --> 00:04:50.767
And then -- But I wanted
to do the Sonny part, too,

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:52.634
and I read for it.

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:56.334
But I think Francis was
pretty set on Jimmy Caan, too.

00:04:56.334 --> 00:04:59.801
But, you know,
they let me read, so...

00:04:59.801 --> 00:05:02.033
-We actually have
the audition of you

00:05:02.033 --> 00:05:03.567
doing the audition
for Sonny Corleone.

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:05.334
I don't know when the last time
you saw this,

00:05:05.334 --> 00:05:06.801
but it's a few seconds.

00:05:06.801 --> 00:05:08.400
Take a look at this.
-Here's Robert De Niro.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.334
-You're gonna take both of them
out on me.

00:05:10.334 --> 00:05:11.634
You're gonna take them,

00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:13.000
and you know what
they're gonna do to you?

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.434
You get his brains
all over your nice

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:15.901
new Ivy League suit, Michael.

00:05:15.901 --> 00:05:17.267
That's what happens.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:19.200
You know you can do it?
All right. You prove it to me.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.000
You can do it?
Go ahead. Prove it.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.100
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.400
-Yeah.
[ Imitating De Niro ]

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.934
Ah, I love it.

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:32.534
[ Laughs ]

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:33.767
Alright. Here's another myth.

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:36.100
In the 1988 movie "Big,"

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.868
you were originally cast
as Josh,

00:05:37.868 --> 00:05:40.000
the role that ended up
going to Tom Hanks.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.667
Is that true?

00:05:42.667 --> 00:05:43.968
-Yes.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:47.133
-Wow. This is unbelievable.
-But we had a thing --

00:05:47.133 --> 00:05:51.467
an issue with the negotiations,
so it went the way it went.

00:05:51.467 --> 00:05:52.901
But that was fine. You know.

00:05:52.901 --> 00:05:54.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:56.334
-Alright.
How about this one here?

00:05:56.334 --> 00:05:58.801
One of the most iconic lines
in the movie,

00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:00.234
"You talkin' to me?",

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:02.667
was not actually in the script
of "Taxi Driver."

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:05.901
Did you ad-lib that line?

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:07.934
-More or less, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:10.267
-Is that the line
that people yell out to you

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:12.033
the most when they see you?

00:06:12.033 --> 00:06:16.000
-Sometimes, yes.
From odd places.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.734
You know, a bunch of kids
pull up next to me in the --

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:21.200
I remember years ago in L.A.
down in the Valley

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:23.100
or somewhere in Culver City.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.901
Kids pull up next to me
at a red light, and they say,

00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:26.167
"You talkin' to me?"

00:06:26.167 --> 00:06:29.367
I don't know how they
recognized me or whatever.

00:06:29.367 --> 00:06:31.667
-[ Laughs ]
Do you ever answer them back?

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:32.801
-No.

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:35.334
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.334 --> 00:06:36.801
-"I'm not talking to you."

00:06:36.801 --> 00:06:38.801
That is Robert De Niro
right there, everybody.

00:06:38.801 --> 00:06:41.367
He's the greatest.
-[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:44.434
The 20th anniversary
of the Tribeca Film Festival

00:06:44.434 --> 00:06:45.968
kicks off this week.

